comments.message:
The app uses hash history, But the `Login.vue` code reads the browser URL search params directly:
```
            const url = new URL(window.location.href);
            const nextPath = url.searchParams.get('next');
            this.handleNextPathRedirect(nextPath);
```

This does not work for for hash-router URLs like /#/login?next=/redfish/v1/..., where window.location.search is empty and the query is available through this.$route.query.next.

Recommended fix: use Vue Router query first, then fall back to window.location.search for bmcweb-style redirects:
```
const nextPath =
  this.$route.query.next ||
  new URL(window.location.href).searchParams.get('next');
```

Logged-in `/login?next=/redfish/v1/...` users are sent to `/`, not the Redfish target.
the stated goal is to preserve Redfish redirects. 

Recommended fix: `if to.path === '/login'` and the user is already logged in, inspect the same next target and redirect to it when it is a safe Redfish path:
```
function allowRouterToNavigate(to, next, currentUserRole) {
  if (to.path === '/login' && store.getters['authentication/isLoggedIn']) {
    next('/');
    return;
  }
```

The guard now inspects next= for already-authenticated users:

  if (to.path === '/login' && store.getters['authentication/isLoggedIn']) {
    if (isRedfishRedirectPath(to.query.next)) {
      next(false);
      window.location.assign(`${window.location.origin}${to.query.next}`);
    } else {
      next('/');
    }
    return;
  }

Safe Redfish paths trigger a full-page navigation via
window.location.assign(); all other cases use next('/').

while it makes sense that the Login component is special, it seems like we can centralize redirect logic (similar logic is needed in the router for `/login`.

Consider adding one safe helper and use it both from the router and the Login.vue:
```
function isRedfishRedirectPath(path) {
  return path === '/redfish/v1' || path?.startsWith('/redfish/v1/');
}

function redirectToRedfishOrHome(path, router) {
  if (isRedfishRedirectPath(path)) {
    window.location.assign(`${window.location.origin}${path}`);
  } else {
    router.push('/');
  }
}
```

place within router or in a Redfish utility.

Created src/utilities/redfishRedirect.js with two exported helpers:

  export function isRedfishRedirectPath(path) {
    if (!path) return false;
    return path === '/redfish/v1' || path.startsWith('/redfish/v1/');
  }

  export function redirectToRedfishOrHome(path, router) {
    if (isRedfishRedirectPath(path)) {
      window.location.assign(`${window.location.origin}${path}`);
    } else {
      router.push('/');
    }
  }

Both router/index.js and Login.vue import from this shared utility.

isRedfishRedirectPath() now accepts both the service root and
sub-resources:

  return path === '/redfish/v1' || path.startsWith('/redfish/v1/');

`Login.vue` reads next from both `$route.query` and `window.location.search`. The router guard only uses `to.query.next`.

For bmcweb-style URLs like `/login?next=/redfish/v1/Systems` (query outside the hash), an already-logged-in user gets sent to `/` instead of the Redfish URL. This is exactly scenario #3 in the commit message's manual test plan — it may fail depending on URL format.

Recommendation: Extract a shared helper:
```
export function getNextRedirectPath(route) {
  const fromQuery = route?.query?.next;
  if (typeof fromQuery === 'string') return fromQuery;
  return new URL(window.location.href).searchParams.get('next');
}

```
Use it in both `Login.vue` and the router guard.

`isRedfishRedirectPath()` uses a string prefix check only. Paths with .. segments pass validation but normalize to non-Redfish destinations.
`/redfish/v1/Systems/../../../../admin` resolves to `/admin`
`/redfish/v1/../admin` resolves to `/redfish/admin`

Recommendation: Validate the normalized pathname:
```
export function isRedfishRedirectPath(path) {
  if (typeof path !== 'string' || !path.startsWith('/')) return false;
  try {
    const { pathname } = new URL(path, 'https://example.com');
    return pathname === '/redfish/v1' || pathname.startsWith('/redfish/v1/');
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

